- Story & Context: The legend began that Engine 49, the oldest and most temperamental locomotive on the inter-district line, wasn't just metal, but a vessel for the collective rage of all who had ever suffered its delays and breakdowns; it was said to carry the ghosts of angry commuters. One evening, during a sudden, unexplainable system-wide blackout, the engine roared to life on its own, its horn blaring a distorted, almost musical cry that echoed the clandestine jams of the night trains, becoming the rebellious symbol for the burgeoning Trackswath movement. It was a roar of defiance, a promise kept: "No more last calls, only new beginnings, forged in the iron heart of our failings."
